"An interaction overview diagram coordinates referenced interactions using activity-style control flow.\n\nUse when: Connect several detailed interaction scenarios into one overview. Explain branching between reusable interaction fragments.\n\nAvoid when: One short sequence can express the complete interaction directly.\n\nVisual grammar: Interaction-use nodes reference separately defined collaborations. Activity-style decisions, forks, and joins control progression between them. Initial and final nodes identify the overview’s entry and completion.\n\nBuild: Identify the overall scenario and its reusable interaction references. Arrange references in the normal completion order. Add guarded alternatives and synchronization between interaction uses. Keep references compact and place detailed messages in linked diagrams rather than inside overview nodes.\n\nCheck: Every interaction reference resolves to a named detailed view. Control nodes do not imply message semantics they do not define. Alternative and concurrent paths have intentional completion behavior. Reference names, guards, and connector bends remain visually separate.\n\nPitfalls: Embedding full sequence diagrams until the overview loses its purpose. Confusing a referenced interaction with an ordinary atomic action.\n\nExample: A booking overview invokes Authenticate, then ReserveInventory and AuthorizePayment, then either Confirm or Compensate.\n\nAlternatives: Sequence diagram, Activity diagram."
